    "https://jobs.uncw.edu/postings/41296": {
      "title": "Director of Clinical Education - Doctor of Physical Therapy Program",
      "description": "University of North Carolina Wilmington Employment Opportunities | Director of Clinical Education - Doctor of Physical Therapy Program Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Applicant FAQ's Employee Benefits Perks and Discounts Employee Engagement Hub Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Director of Clinical Education - Doctor of Physical Therapy Program Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Prior to the official appointment to the university, the candidate(s) selected for hire must be able to demonstrate eligibility to teach assigned courses by successfully meeting the credentialing requirements based on official transcripts and other documentation as required. See below for required materials and instructions: Curriculum Vita Cover Letter (3-4 pages in length; please address how you meet the minimum requirements and the preferred qualifications for this position) List of 3 Professional References Combined Unofficial Graduate Transcripts (official transcripts will be required prior to employment) Posting Details Posting Details Position Title Director of Clinical Education - Doctor of Physical Therapy Program External Link to Posting https://jobs.uncw.edu/postings/41294 College College of Health and Human Services - 315 College Dept/School School of Health & Applied Human Sciences - 31530 Vacancy Number 16F-824 Position Number 741 Location of Workplace Main UNCW Campus Brief Summary of Work for this Position The School of Health and Applied Human Sciences ( SHAHS ) within the College of Health and Human Services at the University of North Carolina Wilmington ( UNCW ) seeks an outstanding colleague for a full-time, 12-month, non-tenure track position at the rank of Clinical Assistant Professor or Clinical Associate Professor in the School’s Doctor of Physical Therapy program. This position is expected to start Spring 2027. Rank at appointment is dependent on qualifications relative to University and College standards. Clinical Track faculty are initially appointed on a contract for between 3-5 years, renewable based on performance. Clinical Track faculty are eligible to apply for promotion to higher Clinical Track ranks. Moreover, depending on rank and yearly workload allocation, the successful candidate may conduct research, publish articles, write grants, mentor students, and engage in service to the program, school, college, university, community, and profession. The Director of Clinical Education ( DCE ) is a faculty member responsible for leading and overseeing all aspects of the clinical education component of a physical therapy program. The DCE develops, coordinates, and evaluates clinical learning experiences; manages partnerships with clinical education sites; places and supports students during clinical rotations; and serves as the primary liaison between the academic program and clinical partners. The position also involves assessing student performance, maintaining accreditation and compliance requirements, supporting curriculum development, and ensuring the availability of high-quality clinical learning opportunities. In addition, the DCE provides leadership in clinical faculty development, clinical site management, program assessment, and continuous improvement of the clinical education program. This is a non-remote position. The candidate will work in a collaborative interdisciplinary academic setting. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements Must hold, or be eligible for, licensure as a physical therapist in the State of North Carolina and maintain active licensure throughout employment. Maintain a valid driver’s license and be available for travel as required. Minimum of 3 years’ experience as a licensed physical therapist. Earned Doctorate in Physical Therapy ( DPT ) or be a licensed PT (with an entry-level BSPT or MSPT ) and possess a terminal degree (tDPT, PhD, DSc, EdD). Note: Applicants who are ABD at the time of application will be considered. Individuals who are engaged in terminal degrees ABD must earn their terminal degree, prior to the program’s AFC submission in 2030. Preferred Education, Knowledge, Skills & Experience In addition to the minimum qualifications, preference will be given to candidates who possess the following: Prior experience as a Director of Clinical Education, Academic Coordinator of Clinical Education, Site Coordinator of Clinical Education, or comparable leadership role in clinical education. Demonstrated knowledge of clinical education models, clinical placement processes, student assessment, site development, and regulatory/accreditation expectations relevant to physical therapist education, including CAPTE standards. Experience developing, administering, assessing, and documenting clinical education programs. Strong communication, organizational, interpersonal, problem-solving, conflict-resolution, and student-support skills. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively with faculty, clinical partners, students, administrators, and community stakeholders. Knowledge of legislative, regulatory, legal, ethical, and professional practice issues affecting clinical education, students, clinical sites, and the physical therapy profession. Prior teaching experience in a physical therapist education program and/or administrative leadership experience in clinical practice. Current or prior participation in clinical practice, particularly in roles that inform clinical education, clinical mentoring, or student supervision. Completion of the APTA Credentialed Clinical Instructor Program or comparable clinical education training. Advanced clinical expertise, such as completion of a residency or fellowship, ABPTS board certification, or other evidence of advanced clinical competence. Earned academic doctoral degree or evidence of progress toward doctoral-level preparation in a relevant field. Record of scholarly productivity, professional service, and/or leadership commensurate with academic rank. Interest or experience in designing and conducting independent or collaborative scholarship related to clinical education, physical therapist education, or related areas. Active involvement in professional activities at the local, state, regional, or national level. Membership in the American Physical Therapy Association or another relevant professional organization. Knowledge of educational, management, assessment, and adult learning theories and their application to clinical education. Proficiency with information systems and technology used in clinical education, assessment, communication, data management, and program administration, such as Exxat, Qualtrics, learning management systems, databases, word processing, and email. Ability to meet faculty expectations consistent with CAPTE requirements, program needs, and applicable UNCW faculty policies. Experience using emerging technologies, including artificial intelligence, to support teaching, assessment, clinical education management, documentation, or professional practice. Required Certifications or Licensure Must hold, or be eligible for, licensure as a physical therapist in the State of North Carolina and maintain active licensure throughout employment. Maintain a valid driver’s license and be available for travel as required. Primary Function of Organizational Unit The Doctor of Physical Therapy ( DPT ) program is a 30-month professional program designed to prepare graduates for licensure and clinical practice across the lifespan as physical therapists. Through a competency-based curriculum grounded in best practice, students develop the evidence-based knowledge, clinical skills, and professional judgment needed to deliver patient-centered physical therapy services across healthcare settings, including rural communities in North Carolina. The year-round curriculum requires a total of 111 credits: 90 credits of foundational and professional didactic education, 2 credits (60 hours) of integrated clinical education ( ICE ), and 19 credits (38 weeks) of full-time clinical education. ICE placements help students understand the range of physical therapy practice settings. Full-time clinical education follows a scaffolded model that progressively builds competence and proficiency in delivering evidence-based, hands-on, patient-centered care across the lifespan. These experiences total approximately 1,520 hours and are completed under the supervision of licensed physical therapists. Clinical placements are available throughout the continental United States, allowing students to explore both clinical interests and geographic preferences. The eight-semester cohort begins each summer and provides a structured sequence of coursework, laboratories, and cumulative practical examinations that prepares students for advanced clinical training. The DPT program is currently housed in the School of Health and Applied Human Sciences ( SHAHS ). The DPT program is currently housed in the School of Health and Applied Human Sciences ( SHAHS ). The SHAHS has six undergraduate degree programs: Public Health Exercise Science Healthful Living and Fitness Education Tourism, Recreation, & Sport Recreation Therapy Respiratory Therapy (Face-to-face and fully online option) The SHAHS also has graduate degrees in: Athletic Training Healthcare Administration (100% Online program) Gerontology with 4+1 options: (Bachelors / Masters) with Public Health / Gerontology Recreation Therapy / Gerontology Exercise Science / Gerontology Physical Therapy (in preparation) The SHAHS also supports: Master of Arts in Teaching ( MAT ) through the Watson College of Education Master of Education concentration in Physical Education and Health through the Watson College of Education Minors are offered in: Gerontology Health & Wellness Coaching Whole Health & Wellness Studies Tourism, Recreation, & Sport Yoga Studies Assistance Dog Training The SHAHS is also home to the Physical Activity and Wellness ( WPA /L) program which delivers courses that fulfill University Studies requirements. The SHAHS is located in Veterans Hall which includes many state-of-the-art instructional and lab spaces utilized by the Exercise Science program. College/School Information The College of Health and Human Services was created in July 2010 to provide UNCW with a new mechanism to link and focus its health-related research, professional continuing education, and health knowledge dissemination and application. In the formative stage of development, the CHHS is currently home to three professional schools: School of Health and Applied Human Sciences, School of Nursing, and School of Social Work. This is an outstanding opportunity to join a dynamic College that seeks to be nationally recognized for its inter-professional collaboration, community engagement and impact, and implementation science. The faculty, staff and students of the CHHS are committed to the pursuit of excellence and enhancing health and quality of life across the lifespan for individuals, families and communities in southeastern North Carolina and beyond. University Information The University of North Carolina Wilmington, the state’s coastal university, is dedicated to the integration of teaching, mentoring, research, and service. UNCW is distinguished by its commitment to putting students first, supporting world-class faculty and staff, delivering transformative learning experiences, and leveraging its unique proximity to the ocean. Recognized nationally for scholarly excellence and affordability, UNCW offers a powerful academic experience that stimulates creative inquiry and critical thinking and reflects emerging industries and interdisciplinary innovation. A public institution with nearly 20,000 students, UNCW offers a full range of baccalaureate- and graduate-level programs, as well as doctoral degrees in educational leadership, marine biology, nursing practice and psychology; and many distance learning options, including clinical research, an accelerated RN-to- BSN program, an Executive M.B.A. program structured for working professionals, and the nation’s first bachelor’s degree program focused on coastal engineering. The university’s efforts to advance research and scholarly activities have earned UNCW the elevated designation of “Doctoral Universities: High Research Activity” institution (R2 University) by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education. Founded as Wilmington College in 1947, UNCW moved to its present location in the heart of New Hanover County in 1961. The 660-acre campus is only minutes from Wrightsville Beach and historic downtown Wilmington and features Georgian architecture and designated conservation areas. Wilmington International Airport ( ILM ) provides commercial air service to cities both domestic and abroad and is just six miles from the UNCW campus. UNCW has been part of the University of North Carolina System since 1969. EEO Statement UNCW does not discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment because of his or her race, sex (such as gender, gender identity, marital status, childbirth, and pregnancy), age, color, national origin (including ethnicity), religion, disability, sexual orientation, political affiliation, veteran status, military service member status, genetic information, or relationship to other university constituents – except where sex, age, or ability represent bona fide educational or occupational qualifications or where marital status is a statutorily established eligibility criterion for State funded employee benefit programs. Eligibility for Employment Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. UNC Wilmington participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Number of Openings Single Incumbent Special Notes to Applicants Prior to the official appointment to the university, the candidate(s) selected for hire must be able to demonstrate eligibility to teach assigned courses by successfully meeting the credentialing requirements based on official transcripts and other documentation as required. See below for required materials and instructions: Curriculum Vita Cover Letter (3-4 pages in length; please address how you meet the minimum requirements and the preferred qualifications for this position) List of 3 Professional References Combined Unofficial Graduate Transcripts (official transcripts will be required prior to employment) Position Type Permanent Job Posting Date 10/27/2026 Job Close Date 11/26/2026 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vita List of 3 Professional References Combined Unofficial Graduate Transcripts Optional Documents Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this faculty opportunity with UNCW? *UNCW or College Website *Direct Contact from UNCW Faculty or Staff *Professional Organization or Association *Listserv or Professional Job Board *UNCW Social Media *Higher Ed Publication (such as Chronicle of Higher *Education, Inside Higher Ed, Higher Ed Jobs) *HERC (Higher Education Recruitment Consortium) *Other (Open Ended Question) If you responded \"other\" above, please indicate where you learned of the faculty opportunity at UNCW: (Open Ended Question) 601 S. College Rd | Wilmington, NC 28403 © The University of North Carolina Wilmington. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.uncw.edu/postings/41278": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Communication Studies",
      "description": "University of North Carolina Wilmington Employment Opportunities | Lecturer in Communication Studies Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Applicant FAQ's Employee Benefits Perks and Discounts Employee Engagement Hub Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Lecturer in Communication Studies Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Prior to the official appointment to the university, the candidate selected for hire must be able to demonstrate eligibility to teach assigned courses by successfully meeting the credentialing requirements based on official transcripts and other documentation as required. Applications will be accepted until 11:59PM EST on the posting closing date. To apply, please complete the online application available at https://jobs.uncw.edu/ and attach the following materials as Adobe PDF documents: Cover Letter /Letter of Application that includes a 1-to-2-page statement addressing the qualifications cited above and addressed to the Search Committee Curriculum Vita Teaching Philosophy Portfolio/Work Sample : Potential content may include any of the following: sample syllabus, assignments, feedback to students, digital creativity, etc. Additional materials may be requested from applicants invited to interview for the position. Please note that a minimum of three references are required for all applications. The reference contact information you provide may be used to request reference letters during the screening process. Letters of application without the requested components will not meet the application requirements. Questions about the position or the online application process should be directed to CHSSA Recruitment Specialist Jennifer Itell itellj@uncw.edu . Posting Details Posting Details Position Title Lecturer in Communication Studies External Link to Posting https://jobs.uncw.edu/postings/41245 College College of Humanities, Social Sciences, and The Arts - 321 College Dept/School Communication Studies - 32241 Vacancy Number 16F-822 Position Number 740 Location of Workplace Main UNCW Campus Brief Summary of Work for this Position The Department of Communication Studies at the University of North Carolina Wilmington invites applications for a position as a nine-month non-tenure-track lecturer to begin August 2027. This is a one-year position that is renewable annually, contingent upon satisfactory performance, departmental needs, and the availability of funding. Lecturers are expected to teach a 4/4 load. The teaching focus will be a combination of face-to-face and online asynchronous teaching in applied organizational communication. This will include business communication, digital communication, public speaking, and similar courses. Additional teaching opportunities in rhetorical studies may also be available. Additional responsibilities include service to the department, campus, and community. The successful candidate will demonstrate experience in taking the discipline into the market place through workshops, job talks, and other efforts. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements MS or MA in Communication Studies at the time of application Preferred Education, Knowledge, Skills & Experience Preference will be given to candidates with: MS or MA with a specialization in applied communication, organizationalcommunication, or closely related areas Demonstrated ability to teach business communication, other applied courses,and digital communication competencies Prior teaching experience and strong desire to teach in face-to-face andasynchronous online modalities Prior experience or strong desire to work with diverse populations and adultlearners Certifications in Adobe, MS Office, Canvas, online education such as Quality Matters Demonstrated ability to bring communication studies into the marketplace and not for profit sectors. Required Certifications or Licensure Primary Function of Organizational Unit The Department of Communication Studies is a large department within the College of Humanities, Social Sciences, and the Arts. We have over 20 full-time faculty who are methodologically diverse and embrace the breadth of the study of human communication and celebrate our roots in the humanities and social sciences. We have approximately550 majors in our BA program and 100 students in our online MA program in Integrated Marketing Communication ( IMC ). This program hosts a biennial conference for IMC scholars, practitioners, and our graduate students. We share a commitment to praxis and applied learning. UNCW is an Adobe Campus and offers dedicated support for faculty to create high impact practices for our students. Our library has a robust maker space, and our department has several application focused classrooms including a production studio for both recording and live performance. We value excellent teaching, creative work and engagement with the department, university, community, and discipline through thoughtful service. We seek energetic and optimistic colleagues interested in fostering and cultivating sustained excellence. We work independently and collaboratively to create powerful learning experiences for our students and to co-create a great place to work. We balance curricular specialization and academic rigor with whole student development and next step readiness. College/School Information The College of Humanities, Social Sciences, and the Arts ( CHSSA ) is home to 14 academic departments and offers a wide variety of undergraduate and graduate degree programs including the Bachelor of Arts, Bachelor of Fine Arts, Bachelor of Music, Master of Arts, Master of Fine Arts, and Master of Public Administration as well as combined bachelor’s/master’s degree programs in Spanish and English. CHSSA also houses the Interdisciplinary Studies program(s), Gender Studies and Research Center and the Academic Resource Center, which offers full-service, professional advising for all CHSSA undergraduate students. In addition, CHSSA offers a full complement of interdisciplinary minors and post baccalaureate certificates including: Africana studies, American studies, Asian studies, classical studies, conflict management and resolution, emergency and disaster management, forensic science, Hispanic studies, journalism, Latin American studies, medical humanities, Middle East and Islamic studies, Native American studies, professional writing and publishing, science and medical writing, and women’s, gender, and sexuality studies. CHSSA contributes significantly to the curriculum that comprises UNCW’s University Studies Program, through which all undergraduates gain a foundation in the liberal arts and develop the transferable skills essential for life in the twenty-first century. CHSSA strives in its undergraduate programs and in its University Study offerings to nurture creative thinking, intellectual curiosity, and academic integrity in students while providing them with a solid foundation of knowledge in their chosen fields. Graduate programs in CHSSA are designed to develop intellectual competence and to prepare the student for careers in business, industry, government, teaching or for further study at the doctoral level. Applied learning is a hallmark of the student experience in CHSSA . Each of CHSSA’s departments requires an applied learning experience as a part of the curriculum for its undergraduate majors. Examples include faculty-directed research, internships, service learning, and capstone courses and projects, all of which challenge students to integrate various strands of knowledge and to apply that knowledge to broader, real-world or real disciplinary situations. Upon completion of their studies, all CHSSA students will have the necessary preparation for rewarding careers, advanced study, effective citizenship, and meaningful lives. CHSSA offers limitless opportunities to engage in collaborative and interdisciplinary research and service while also making a positive societal impact. Come and join our vibrant academic environment that values discovery, innovation, and creativity. University Information The University of North Carolina Wilmington, the state’s coastal university, is dedicated to the integration of teaching, mentoring, research and service. Widely acknowledged for its world-class faculty and staff and continuously recognized at a national level for scholarly excellence and affordability, UNCW offers a powerful academic experience that stimulates creative inquiry and critical thinking. A public institution with nearly 18,000 students, UNCW offers a full range of baccalaureate- and graduate-level programs, as well as doctoral degrees in educational leadership, marine biology, nursing practice and psychology; and many distance learning options, including clinical research, an accelerated RN-to- BSN program, an Executive M.B.A. program structured for working professionals, and the nation’s only bachelor’s degree program focused on coastal engineering. The university’s efforts to advance research and scholarly activities have earned UNCW the elevated designation of “Doctoral Universities: High Research Activity” institution (R2 University) by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education. UNCW has been part of the University of North Carolina System since 1969. EEO Statement UNCW does not discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment because of his or her race, sex (such as gender, gender identity, marital status, childbirth, and pregnancy), age, color, national origin (including ethnicity), religion, disability, sexual orientation, political affiliation, veteran status, military service member status, genetic information, or relationship to other university constituents – except where sex, age, or ability represent bona fide educational or occupational qualifications or where marital status is a statutorily established eligibility criterion for State funded employee benefit programs. Eligibility for Employment Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. UNC Wilmington participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Number of Openings Single Incumbent Special Notes to Applicants Prior to the official appointment to the university, the candidate selected for hire must be able to demonstrate eligibility to teach assigned courses by successfully meeting the credentialing requirements based on official transcripts and other documentation as required. Applications will be accepted until 11:59PM EST on the posting closing date. To apply, please complete the online application available at https://jobs.uncw.edu/ and attach the following materials as Adobe PDF documents: Cover Letter /Letter of Application that includes a 1-to-2-page statement addressing the qualifications cited above and addressed to the Search Committee Curriculum Vita Teaching Philosophy Portfolio/Work Sample : Potential content may include any of the following: sample syllabus, assignments, feedback to students, digital creativity, etc. Additional materials may be requested from applicants invited to interview for the position. Please note that a minimum of three references are required for all applications. The reference contact information you provide may be used to request reference letters during the screening process. Letters of application without the requested components will not meet the application requirements. Questions about the position or the online application process should be directed to CHSSA Recruitment Specialist Jennifer Itell itellj@uncw.edu . Position Type Permanent Job Posting Date 10/26/2026 Job Close Date 12/15/2026 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vita Teaching Philosophy Portfolio/Work Sample Optional Documents Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this opportunity with UNCW? (2026) UNCW Website UNCW LinkedIn Page UNCW X Account (formerly Twitter) UNCW Employee Referral UNCW Instagram Account (UNCW_jobs) UNCW Job Alert Chronical of Higher Education Glassdoor Higher Ed Jobs Indeed Inside Higher Ed North Carolina Job Network Craigslist Career Events/Job Fairs Billboards/Marquee Other If you responded \"other\" above, please indicate where you learned of the faculty opportunity at UNCW: (Open Ended Question) 601 S. College Rd | Wilmington, NC 28403 © The University of North Carolina Wilmington. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
