    "https://jobs.uncw.edu/postings/41272": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Interdisciplinary Studies",
      "description": "University of North Carolina Wilmington Employment Opportunities | Lecturer in Interdisciplinary Studies Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Applicant FAQ's Employee Benefits Perks and Discounts Employee Engagement Hub Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Lecturer in Interdisciplinary Studies Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Prior to the official appointment to the university, the candidate selected for hire must be able to demonstrate eligibility to teach assigned courses by successfully meeting the credentialing requirements based on official transcripts and other documentation as required. Applications will be accepted until 11:59PM EST on the posting closing date. To apply, please complete the online application process available at https://jobs.uncw.edu/ and attach the following materials as Adobe PDF documents (one PDF per document type) including: Cover Letter /Letter of Application that includes a 1-to-2-page statement addressing the qualifications cited in the brief description of work and addressed to the Search Committee Curriculum Vita Teaching Philosophy as it relates to Asynchronous Online Learning Additional materials may be requested from applicants invited to interview for the position. Please note that a minimum of three references is required for all applications in the references section of the application. References may be contacted during the screening process. Letters of application without the requested components will not meet the application requirements. Questions about the position or the online application process should be directed to CHSSA Recruitment Specialist Jennifer Itell @ itellj@uncw.edu . Posting Details Posting Details Position Title Lecturer in Interdisciplinary Studies External Link to Posting https://jobs.uncw.edu/postings/41268 College College of Humanities, Social Sciences, and The Arts - 321 College Dept/School Interdisciplinary Studies - 30340 Vacancy Number 16F-823 Position Number 707 Location of Workplace Main UNCW Campus Brief Summary of Work for this Position Interdisciplinary Studies Programs at the University of North Carolina Wilmington invites applications for a position as a nine-month non-tenure-track lecturer to begin August 2027. This position carries a 4:4 teaching load, comprised of the core classes in the program as well as one course in the successful candidate’s area of expertise, which must align with one of the six concentration tracks in the program. The six concentration tracks include: Enterprise and Economy; Global Challenges and Problems; Justice, Social Institutions, and Human Behavior; Artificial Intelligence and Technology in the Arts and Sciences; Interdisciplinary Arts and Humanities and Human Wellbeing. All core courses for IDS are taught online in both traditional and accelerated formats. The successful candidate will lead continuous improvement efforts to the core courses to meet student needs. In addition, the successful candidate will be an active member of the university through service and departmental engagement. Minimum Education and Experience Requirements MA in Interdisciplinary Studies, Liberal Studies, or another inherently interdisciplinary discipline at time of application At least three years of meaningful experience teaching online, asynchronous, interdisciplinary courses at the University level At least three years of direct experience working with adult learners Commitment to an authentic student experience in an online, asynchronous environment Preferred Education, Knowledge, Skills & Experience PhD in Interdisciplinary Studies, Liberal Studies, or other inherently interdisciplinary discipline Experience teaching in an accelerated format Required Certifications or Licensure Primary Function of Organizational Unit Interdisciplinary Studies Programs in the College of Humanities, Social Sciences, and the Arts houses the Interdisciplinary BA and MA programs, the Gender Studies Research Center, and is a hub for the Interdisciplinary Minors in the College. The unit works collaboratively to align priorities around student success. The IDS BA degree is a rapidly growing major with close to 200 majors across modalities. Students take three common core classes, have three additional core competencies in Critical Reasoning, Intellectual Inquiry, and Global Perspectives, and then choose from one of six concentration tracks in courses across the University. The six concentration tracks include: Enterprise and Economy; Global Challenges and Problems; Justice, Social Institutions, and Human Behavior; Artificial Intelligence and Technology in the Arts and Sciences; Interdisciplinary Arts and Humanities; and Human Wellbeing. We have recently launched the program in an asynchronous online accelerated modality where we emphasize authentic student-faculty engagement for adult learners. The primary driver for the IDS degree is to reduce barriers to high-quality, career-relevant education by embracing the range of experiences of our students. We support flexible pathways to graduation through curriculum and modality, always with the student experience in mind. College/School Information The College of Humanities, Social Sciences, and the Arts ( CHSSA ) is home to 14 academic departments and offers a wide variety of undergraduate and graduate degree programs including the Bachelor of Arts, Bachelor of Fine Arts, Bachelor of Music, Master of Arts, Master of Fine Arts, and Master of Public Administration as well as combined bachelor’s/master’s degree programs in Spanish and English. CHSSA also houses the Interdisciplinary Studies program(s), Gender Studies and Research Center and the Academic Resource Center, which offers full-service, professional advising for all CHSSA undergraduate students. In addition, CHSSA offers a full complement of interdisciplinary minors and post baccalaureate certificates including: Africana studies, American studies, Asian studies, classical studies, conflict management and resolution, emergency and disaster management, forensic science, Hispanic studies, journalism, Latin American studies, medical humanities, Middle East and Islamic studies, Native American studies, professional writing and publishing, science and medical writing, and women’s, gender, and sexuality studies. CHSSA contributes significantly to the curriculum that comprises UNCW’s University Studies Program, through which all undergraduates gain a foundation in the liberal arts and develop the transferable skills essential for life in the twenty-first century. CHSSA strives in its undergraduate programs and in its University Study offerings to nurture creative thinking, intellectual curiosity, and academic integrity in students while providing them with a solid foundation of knowledge in their chosen fields. Graduate programs in CHSSA are designed to develop intellectual competence and to prepare the student for careers in business, industry, government, teaching or for further study at the doctoral level. Applied learning is a hallmark of the student experience in CHSSA . Each of CHSSA’s departments requires an applied learning experience as a part of the curriculum for its undergraduate majors. Examples include faculty-directed research, internships, service learning, and capstone courses and projects, all of which challenge students to integrate various strands of knowledge and to apply that knowledge to broader, real-world or real disciplinary situations. Upon completion of their studies, all CHSSA students will have the necessary preparation for rewarding careers, advanced study, effective citizenship, and meaningful lives. CHSSA offers limitless opportunities to engage in collaborative and interdisciplinary research and service while also making a positive societal impact. Come and join our vibrant academic environment that values discovery, innovation, and creativity. University Information The University of North Carolina Wilmington, the state’s coastal university, is dedicated to the integration of teaching, mentoring, research, and service. UNCW is distinguished by its commitment to putting students first, supporting world-class faculty and staff, delivering transformative learning experiences, and leveraging its unique proximity to the ocean. Recognized nationally for scholarly excellence and affordability, UNCW offers a powerful academic experience that stimulates creative inquiry and critical thinking and reflects emerging industries and interdisciplinary innovation. A public institution with nearly 20,000 students, UNCW offers a full range of baccalaureate- and graduate-level programs, as well as doctoral degrees in educational leadership, marine biology, nursing practice and psychology; and many distance learning options, including clinical research, an accelerated RN-to- BSN program, an Executive M.B.A. program structured for working professionals, and the nation’s first bachelor’s degree program focused on coastal engineering. The university’s efforts to advance research and scholarly activities have earned UNCW the elevated designation of “Doctoral Universities: High Research Activity” institution (R2 University) by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education. Founded as Wilmington College in 1947, UNCW moved to its present location in the heart of New Hanover County in 1961. The 660-acre campus is only minutes from Wrightsville Beach and historic downtown Wilmington and features Georgian architecture and designated conservation areas. Wilmington International Airport ( ILM ) provides commercial air service to cities both domestic and abroad and is just six miles from the UNCW campus. UNCW has been part of the University of North Carolina System since 1969. EEO Statement UNCW does not discriminate against any employee or applicant for employment because of his or her race, sex (such as gender, gender identity, marital status, childbirth, and pregnancy), age, color, national origin (including ethnicity), religion, disability, sexual orientation, political affiliation, veteran status, military service member status, genetic information, or relationship to other university constituents – except where sex, age, or ability represent bona fide educational or occupational qualifications or where marital status is a statutorily established eligibility criterion for State funded employee benefit programs. Eligibility for Employment Final candidates are subject to criminal & sex offender background checks. Some vacancies also require credit or motor vehicle checks. UNC Wilmington participates in E-Verify. Federal law requires all employers to verify the identity and employment eligibility of all persons hired to work in the United States. Number of Openings Single Incumbent Special Notes to Applicants Prior to the official appointment to the university, the candidate selected for hire must be able to demonstrate eligibility to teach assigned courses by successfully meeting the credentialing requirements based on official transcripts and other documentation as required. Applications will be accepted until 11:59PM EST on the posting closing date. To apply, please complete the online application process available at https://jobs.uncw.edu/ and attach the following materials as Adobe PDF documents (one PDF per document type) including: Cover Letter /Letter of Application that includes a 1-to-2-page statement addressing the qualifications cited in the brief description of work and addressed to the Search Committee Curriculum Vita Teaching Philosophy as it relates to Asynchronous Online Learning Additional materials may be requested from applicants invited to interview for the position. Please note that a minimum of three references is required for all applications in the references section of the application. References may be contacted during the screening process. Letters of application without the requested components will not meet the application requirements. Questions about the position or the online application process should be directed to CHSSA Recruitment Specialist Jennifer Itell @ itellj@uncw.edu . Position Type Permanent Job Posting Date 10/26/2026 Job Close Date 11/30/2026 Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vita Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this opportunity with UNCW? (2026) UNCW Website UNCW LinkedIn Page UNCW X Account (formerly Twitter) UNCW Employee Referral UNCW Instagram Account (UNCW_jobs) UNCW Job Alert Chronical of Higher Education Glassdoor Higher Ed Jobs Indeed Inside Higher Ed North Carolina Job Network Craigslist Career Events/Job Fairs Billboards/Marquee Other If you responded \"other\" above, please indicate where you learned of the faculty opportunity at UNCW: (Open Ended Question) 601 S. College Rd | Wilmington, NC 28403 © The University of North Carolina Wilmington. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
